NAME
    awk - pattern-directed field processing (a small subset)

SYNOPSIS
    awk [-F c] 'program' [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
    Reads its input a line (record) at a time, splits each record into
    fields and runs a one-rule program against it. The input is the
    files named, read one after the other as one stream (globs expand,
    "-" is stdin), or stdin when none is given. awk is /BIN/AWK. stdin
    is the shell's < file or pipe (see shell), else the console up to
    Ctrl-D.

    The program is one argument in single or double quotes; awk strips
    them itself (the shell does not). An unquoted program ends at the
    first space. It has the form

        [/regex/] [{ print items }]

    pattern    /regex/ selects the lines matching a basic regular
               expression (. * + ? ^ $, see regex); no pattern selects
               every line. A pattern with no action prints the line.
    action     { print items } prints one line; {} or {print} prints
               the whole record.

    Items are separated by commas or spaces; either way one space is
    printed between two items:
        $0          the whole record
        $1 .. $N    a field by number (a missing field is empty)
        $NF         the last field
        NF          the number of fields in the record
        NR          the record number, counting from 1 over all files
        "text"      a string, printed as it is
    Anything else in the action is skipped.

    Fields split on runs of spaces and tabs. With -F c they split on
    every c instead, so empty fields are kept.

OPTIONS
    -F c    the single character c separates fields (-F: or -F :)

LIMITS
    One rule, print only: no BEGIN/END, printf, variables or
    expressions. 40 fields per record; records are cut at 255
    characters, the program at 127, the regex at 79, the action at 119.
    The whole argument tail is 127 characters.

EXAMPLES
    awk '{print $2}' /FRUIT.TXT        the second field of each line
    awk '/red/' /FRUIT.TXT             the lines containing "red"
    awk '{print $1, $NF}' /FRUIT.TXT   the first and the last field
    awk '{print NR ":" $0}' /FRUIT.TXT numbered: "1 : apple 3 red"
    awk -F e '/red/ {print $2}' /FRUIT2.TXT
                                       split on the letter e

NOTES
    Each file ends a line, so the last line of one file never joins the
    first line of the next.
    Removed from the P8X version: input from a pipe or a < redirect
    (Y1/OS has neither yet). New: several files, globs and "-".
    Ported from P8X os/commands/awk.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
